5.vii 
   No. The relative concentration error is not uniform over the full concentration range.
   The ADC uses uniformly spaced voltage levels, but the relationship used to convert voltage,
   back into concentration is nonlinear. Consequently, the same voltage error can produce
   different concentration errors at different concentrations. The quantization error also 
   depends on where the true voltage falls within a quantization bin. The error is small near
   the midpoint and larger near the boundaries. As concentration increases, the voltage passes
   through succesive bins, producing the repeated rises and falls observed in the error curves.

5. viii
    I recomend the 12-bit ADC. On the tests with concentration from 1 to 100 ppm in increments
    of 0.1 ppm, its maximum relative concentration error was approximately 0.169%, compared
    with 0.609% for the 10 bit ADC and 2.703% for the 8bit ADC. It also has the finest voltage
    quantization at approximately 1.22mv and it produced the lowest mean relative concentration
    error of approximatly 0.0436%. The 12bit ADC minmized the concentration measurment error
    compared to the others, it theirfore is the more accurate sampler. This makes sense as the 
    more resolution you have the closer you are to the original signal.